{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/1/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2faf500b\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nRemove each fracture marker line and push the surrounding object pixels one cell away from it.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid with 8-connected foreground objects made from a body color and a rarer marker color.\n- Each object has a non-square bbox; the marker pixels form one complete fracture line across the shorter bbox axis.\n\nRule\n- Erase all marker pixels.\n- For a wide object, each row has one marker; body pixels left of it shift one cell left and body pixels right of it shift one cell right.\n- For a tall object, each column has one marker; body pixels above it shift one cell up and body pixels below it shift one cell down.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker line is a crack through the object, so the two sides separate by one pixel perpendicular to the crack.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"15660dd6\" lines=\"33-54\">\n<description>\nCombine each column's repeated template mask with its single solid color swatch.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a table of framed panels separated by background rows/columns.\n- The leftmost column gives one solid row-label color for each panel row.\n- In each panel column, exactly one panel has a solid non-background interior,\n  while the other panels share the same mask drawn in one template color on\n  the background.\n\nRule:\n- For every panel column, find the row containing the solid interior swatch.\n- Rebuild one output panel using that row's left label as the frame color.\n- Fill the repeated template mask with the swatch color and leave the mask\n  background cells as background; separate rebuilt panels with one\n  background column.\n\nKey insight:\nThe table encodes a colorized stencil per column: the solid panel supplies\nthe paint color and frame label, while the duplicate template panels supply\nthe shape mask.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad173014\" lines=\"97-111\">\n<description>\nRecolor each framed interior mask with the color from the next red frame clockwise around the blue connector cycle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains hollow red square frames connected pairwise by blue line components.\n- The blue components connect the frames into one cycle, with every frame touching exactly two blue components.\n- Each frame interior contains background plus exactly one non-background color mask.\n\nRule\n- Sort the red frames clockwise by their centers around the cycle.\n- Keep each frame's interior mask shape in place, but recolor it with the interior color from the next clockwise frame.\n- Leave the red frames, blue connectors, background, and interior background holes unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The colored masks do not move; the blue cycle gives the order in which their colors rotate.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"981571dc\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nReconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.\n- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.\n\nRule:\n- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.\n- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.\n- When multiple mirrors yield candidates, use the majority color.\n\nKey insight: The reflection axes are not given — they must be deduced from the surviving pixels by finding the axes that best preserve the existing symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"136b0064\" lines=\"25-39\">\n<description>\nDraw a polyline by decoding 3x3 glyph instructions read across a divided grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- Left of a full-height vertical divider: 3x3 colored glyphs on bg.\n- Right of divider: a single gray (5) start pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the divider; shift the gray start into the left canvas.\n- Read glyphs in column-major order (left→right, top→bottom).\n- Each glyph's shape (pixel area) encodes a segment: area 4 → down 2, area 5 → right 3, area 6 → left 4, area 7 → left 2.\n- Append segments sequentially, stepping down one cell between each.\n\nKey insight: Glyph pixel count (area) uniquely determines both segment length and direction — the shape itself is the instruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3dc255db\" lines=\"37-53\">\n<description>\nEject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\n\nRule:\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\n\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d35bdbdc\" lines=\"120-134\">\n<description>\nTransfer center colors between bordered sprites matched by color, keeping only path-connected ones.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, grey (5) path network, and several small \"bordered sprites\" each having a border color and a single center pixel of a different color.\n- Grey path forms a one-stroke trail under 8-connectivity; its start/end cells touch exactly two destination sprites via ports.\n\nRule:\n- Destination sprites are determined by the grey trail's two endpoints.\n- For each destination, find the source sprite whose border color equals the destination's center color.\n- Replace the destination's center pixel with the source's center color.\n- Delete all non-destination sprites; grey paths and destinations remain.\n\nKey insight: Center color of each destination acts as a lookup key into the border colors of other sprites, enabling indirect color transfer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9720b24f\" lines=\"34-46\">\n<description>\nDelete each foreign-color object whose bounding box is contained by a larger object of another color.\n\nInput structure\n- The black pixels are the empty canvas, and every other color forms one or more 8-connected monochromatic objects.\n- Some larger foreground objects act as loose U/C-like containers around smaller objects of different colors.\nRule\n- Find all 8-connected monochromatic non-black objects.\n- Recolor an object to black exactly when its bounding box is contained in the bounding box of a larger object of another color.\n- Keep all other pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\n- The removable objects are identified by containment in another color's rectangular extent, not by their own color or shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7666fa5d\" lines=\"12-26\">\n<description>\nFill the parallelogram-shaped region between parallel anti-diagonal line segments with red.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- One non-bg color forms short diagonal segments, each lying on a single anti-diagonal (r+c = const).\n- Segments come in parallel pairs bounding a region to be filled.\n\nRule:\n- For each main-diagonal axis (r−c = const), collect anti-diagonal segments whose span covers that axis.\n- Sort those segments by their anti-diagonal index (r+c).\n- Fill all bg cells lying strictly between consecutive segment pairs along that axis with red (color 2).\n\nKey insight: The two diagonal directions decouple — segments sit on anti-diagonals, and filling sweeps along main diagonals between them, forming parallelogram regions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\" lines=\"13-26\">\n<description>\nExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n\nKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\" lines=\"35-46\">\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"342ae2ed\" lines=\"34-49\">\n<description>\nDraw the missing diagonal connector segment between each pair of same-color rectangular objects.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground color appears as exactly two separated solid rectangular 4-connected objects.\n- The two rectangles of each color are diagonally offset with facing corners on a 45-degree line.\n\nRule:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
